## RX preview crash follow-up (2026-09-05)

The deployed first fix passed PIE detection and entered selection. The later
RX log reports an access violation in the plugin DLL's inline
`FEditorViewportClient::UseAppTime()` while drawing the custom preview.
RX rendering headers contain additional show flags, but the available header
set does not establish the precise object-size/offset mismatch. The crash
location is confirmed; the detailed ABI mechanism remains a hypothesis.

RX results now use host `FAssetThumbnail` rendering for both selected assets
and tiles. No plugin custom preview viewport is constructed in this route.
Standard UE retains live previews. The inspector logs scan completion and
result-window creation separately to distinguish scanning from UI failures.

The forced-RX native suite contains five tests, including result-window
construction/closure without a new preview world. This construction test does
not certify delayed tile rendering or actual RX engine drawing; those remain
live validation tasks. The host may generate a static thumbnail for loaded
assets rather than only displaying an existing cached image.

Follow-up logs: `InspectorPreviewFixBuild.log`, `InspectorPreviewFixTests.log`,
and `InspectorPreviewFixFull.log` under `MCPGameProject/Saved/Logs/`.

## RX empty results follow-up (2026-09-05)

The deployed preview fix reaches the results window but logs zero matching
actors. A read-only host query in the same running RX world returned 4,346
actors, a valid player controller, and successful screen deprojection.
Header comparison identifies an unsafe scanner path: stock `TActorIterator`
inlines the active-level filter using `ULevel` fields after RX's inserted
`RXGIClipmapData`. This can filter out actors using incorrect offsets.
The exact filter values in the old DLL have not been observed in a debugger.

RX now enumerates actors through exported `UGameplayStatics` code in the host.
It also reads assigned static-mesh and Niagara assets through reflected
properties, avoiding shifted component fields, and obtains component bounds
through the host. Standard retains its iterator and asset getters. Diagnostic
logs distinguish projection failures, enumeration, each bounds/range filter,
matched actors, and collected asset counts.

RX animation collection avoids the non-reflected `MontageInstances` array,
which also follows inserted RX fields. It reports the host's current active
montage per animation instance; additional concurrent or blending-out
montages may be omitted. Active sequence collection through host sync-group
and ungrouped-player APIs remains enabled.

`ScannerActorsAndAssets` uses a transient world to check region, distance,
hidden-actor filtering, mesh reference recovery, and deduplication across
two actors. Run it in both Standard and forced-RX modes on the stock host.
This fixture does not certify the full screenshot projection path or actual
RX runtime compatibility. Deployment and a fresh RX selection remain the
final live check.

Follow-up logs: `InspectorScannerFixBuild.log`,
`InspectorScannerFixTestsRX.log`, `InspectorScannerFixTestsStandard.log`,
and `InspectorScannerFixFull.log` under `MCPGameProject/Saved/Logs/`.

Scanner checkpoint: UE 5.6.1 build passed; the forced-RX native group passed
6/6 tests. The Standard run passed its applicable tests (the RX-only window
test returns without constructing a window). Both scanner runs found two
fixture actors contributing one deduplicated mesh. The full offline profile
passed all 41 entries; scoped ABI guard, whitespace check, and independent
C++ review passed. Actual RX DLL replacement/restart and live scan are pending.

## Mouse control after selection

After completing a region selection, results open with Shift+F1-style mouse
control. Closing the results keeps the mouse released; click the PIE viewport
to resume gameplay capture. Cancelling the first selection still restores the
original input state. Re-selection temporarily enables selection input again,
then returns to released mouse control on completion or cancellation.

This applies to the configured inspector shortcut (default Ctrl+Alt+I), on
both Standard and RX. It does not change the shortcut. The controller restores
gameplay capture settings, then uses the UE 5.6 mouse-control focus/reset path
instead of immediately recapturing the viewport. No synthetic Shift+F1 input
or persistent game input-mode changes are used.

Mouse-control verification logs: `InspectorMouseReleaseBuild.log`,
`InspectorMouseReleaseTests.log`, and `InspectorMouseReleaseFull.log`.
The existing native suite is regression coverage, not a live PIE mouse/focus
test. Confirm results open/close, re-selection/cancel, and click-to-recapture
after replacing the plugin and restarting RX.

## Persistent results and RX live preview

The results window belongs to the editor tool, independently of a PIE session.
Stopping PIE, changing PIE/SIE mode, or opening an asset editor does not destroy
it. New results replace its content in the same window. Re-selection temporarily
hides the window; completion or cancellation shows it again. After PIE ends,
start PIE again to scan a new region. The saved list remains browsable while
its weak actor references may expire. Explicit Close/X and plugin/editor
shutdown still destroy the window and release its preview resources.

RX's selected-asset live preview uses the engine's native `UViewport` widget,
the same native base as RX's `BW_LivePreView` Blueprint, without depending on
that project asset. The host constructs and ticks its own preview scene and
viewport. It never adds actors to
the project level or saves preview content. Static/skeletal meshes rotate,
animation assets play, and Niagara effects simulate. Tiles remain static on RX
to avoid one live simulation per list item. The previous static-only selected
preview and PIE-coupled window behavior described in historical checkpoints
above have been replaced.

Verification for this follow-up: the UE 5.6.1 editor target built successfully.
